  "body": "The question of whether protein language models provide novel structural insights or merely efficient approximation has critical implications for computational biology. Two paradigms have emerged: evolutionary learning through massive sequence analysis versus physics-based modeling of molecular interactions.\n\nAlphaFold represents the evolutionary approach, achieving unprecedented accuracy with a median GDT score of 92.4 on CASP14, vastly outperforming previous methods that scored around 60. This success stems from learning evolutionary constraints across 87% of the human proteome. Similarly, ESM-2 demonstrates that transformers can predict atomic-resolution structures from sequences alone, without multiple sequence alignments, suggesting models internalize evolutionary patterns at scale.\n\nHowever, systematic analysis reveals crucial limitations. Protein language models fail dramatically on intrinsically disordered regions, where AlphaFold assigns low confidence scores (pLDDT < 50) to 37% of disordered residues. This failure occurs precisely where evolutionary sequence conservation provides insufficient information and where physics-based approaches using molecular dynamics would be essential.\n\nThe evidence suggests a clear division of labor: language models excel at approximating evolutionary constraints but cannot predict conformational flexibility that requires understanding physical forces. ESM-2's emergent contact prediction improves with model scale, indicating these systems learn statistical patterns rather than physical principles. The 12M unique sequences used in training represent a vast evolutionary database, but this approach inherently cannot predict novel conformational states absent from evolutionary history.\n\nThis limitation becomes critical for understanding protein function, as many proteins exist in multiple conformational states essential for biological activity. Language models predict single static structures approximating evolutionary consensus, while physics-based methods can model dynamic conformational changes through explicit molecular interactions.",
